-- title:
--   Coordinate-preserving Cartier isomorphism yields a ρ-compatible Dieudonné isomorphism
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, a ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathrm{Zp2}\,p \to W(k)$ (where $\mathrm{Zp2}\,p = W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$), and a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $W(k)/pW(k)$, the quotient of $W(k)$ by the ideal generated by $p$; write $\bar\jmath$ for the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map, the grading homomorphism used throughout.
--
--   The hypotheses on $\Phi$ are: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. the submodules $\Phi.\mathrm{lieZero}$ and $\Phi.\mathrm{lieOne}$ are complementary in the Lie module of $\Phi$ and both are invertible; `hΦ4`, that $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the endomorphism $\Phi.\mathrm{act}(p)$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that $\Phi.\mathrm{lieZero}\ \bar\jmath$ is contained in the kernel of the linear map $\Phi.\mathrm{lieVarpi}$ induced by the linear part of $\varpi$; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces $\Phi.\mathrm{gradedPiece}\ \bar\jmath\ 0$ and $\Phi.\mathrm{gradedPiece}\ \bar\jmath\ 1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ (defined by the eigenvalue conditions $\mathrm{endAct}(\mathrm{act}\,\tau(c))f = \mathrm{homothety}(\bar\jmath(\tau(c))^{p^n})f$ for all Teichmüller lifts $\tau(c)$, $c \in \mathbb F_{p^2}$) are complementary. These data give the graded Cartier module datum $D_\Phi = \Phi.\mathrm{toGradedCartierModuleData}\ \bar\jmath\ \mathrm{hcΦ}$, with underlying module the Cartier module of $\Phi$, Frobenius, integral Verschiebung and the action of $\varpi$. A rigidification datum for $\Phi$ is given: an additive map $r_\Phi : (\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathbb Z_p) \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$, the hypothesis `hLΦ` that $D_\Phi$ admits a canonical $L$-map, and the hypothesis `hrΦ` that for every canonical $L$-map $L$ of $D_\Phi$ the map $r_\Phi$ is a bijection from all of $\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathbb Z_p$ onto the $\eta$-piece $D_\Phi.\mathrm{etaPiece}\ L\ \cdot\ 0$ in degree $0$.
--
--   Next, $\kappa$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ which is a $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra, $\psi : W(k) \to \kappa$ a ring homomorphism, and $t, t'$ are rigidified objects of $\mathrm{Rigidified}\ p\ \Phi\ \kappa$; each consists of a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ over $\kappa$, a natural number $n$, and a tuple of power series $\rho$ over $\kappa/p\kappa$. The hypotheses `ht`, `ht'` state that $t$ and $t'$ are admissible for $(\iota,\psi)$: $X$ is special for the structure map attached to $(\iota,\psi)$, has height $4$, and $\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4n$ from the reduction $t.\Phi\mathrm{bar}\ \psi$ to $t.\mathrm{Xbar}$. Further, $Q$ and $Q'$ are Drinfeld data over $\kappa$ for $K = \mathbb Q_p$ and $\pi = p \in \mathbb Z_p$ (pairs of full lattice families $N_0 \le N_1$ over the prime spectrum, with $p N_1 \subseteq N_0$, open membership loci, invertible modules $T_0, T_1$ with maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to $p$, and comparison maps $u_0, u_1$ into the stalks, subject to the compatibilities in the structure). The hypotheses `hQ`, `hQ'` state that $Q$, respectively $Q'$, is a Cartier quadruple for $t$, respectively $t'$, relative to $(\iota, \mathrm{hcΦ}, r_\Phi, \psi)$: $\rho$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules, there are $\kappa$-linear identifications of $T_0$, $T_1$ with $\mathrm{lieZero}$, $\mathrm{lieOne}$ of $X$ carrying $\Pi_0$, $\Pi_1$ to $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$, and at each prime of $\kappa$ the lattices $N_0$, $N_1$ consist exactly of the vectors presented by $\eta$-sections in degree $0$, respectively $1$, over a localisation of $\kappa$, the maps $u_0$, $u_1$ being computed from the associated $\eta$-coordinates (the remaining clauses of the definition are summarised here). The hypothesis `hiso` states that $Q$ and $Q'$ are isomorphic as Drinfeld data, and `hOD`, `hOD'` state that $t.\rho$, respectively $t'.\rho$, is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules from the reduced $\Phi$ to $t.\mathrm{Xbar}$, respectively $t'.\mathrm{Xbar}$.
--
--   All Cartier-theoretic data below are taken over the localisation $\mathrm{Baway}\,(1:\kappa)$ of $\kappa$ at the powers of $1$, with $g = \mathrm{awayHom}\,(1:\kappa)$ the canonical map, and over the corresponding models $t.\mathrm{XS}\ g$, $t'.\mathrm{XS}\ g$ with grading homomorphism $\mathrm{jS}\ \iota\ \psi\ g$. The hypotheses `hc`, `hc'` state that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of these models are complementary, `hcb`, `hcb'` the same for the reductions $t.\mathrm{XbarS}\ g$, $t'.\mathrm{XbarS}\ g$ with grading $\mathrm{jSbar}\ \iota\ \psi\ g$, and `hcΦg` the same for the base change $\mathrm{PhibarS}\ \psi\ g$ of $\Phi$ with grading $\mathrm{jPhiS}\ \iota\ \psi\ g$. Write $D$ and $D'$ for the graded Cartier module data of $t.\mathrm{XS}\ g$ and $t'.\mathrm{XS}\ g$ attached to `hc`, `hc'`. Canonical $L$-maps $L$ for $D$ and $L'$ for $D'$ are given, with the hypotheses `hL`, `hL'`.
--
--   Finally $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$ is a degree, and the following are assumed. `hi`, `hi'`: for every $m$ in the degree-$i$ graded piece of $t.\mathrm{XS}\ g$, respectively $t'.\mathrm{XS}\ g$, the element $\mathrm{endAct}(\varpi)m$ lies in the image of the integral Verschiebung on the Cartier module. $\theta_\eta$ is an additive map from $D.\mathrm{etaPiece}\ L\ \cdot\ i$ to $D'.\mathrm{etaPiece}\ L'\ \cdot\ i$, and `hcoord` states that $\theta_\eta$ preserves rigidified coordinates: for every $z$ in $D.\mathrm{etaPiece}\ L\ \cdot\ i$ and every $v : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathbb Q_p$, the predicate $t.\mathrm{IsEtaSection}$ holds for $(z, v)$ in degree $i$ if and only if $t'.\mathrm{IsEtaSection}$ holds for $(\theta_\eta z, v)$ in degree $i$; here $t.\mathrm{IsEtaSection}$ for $(z,v)$ asserts that $z$ lies in $D.\mathrm{etaPiece}\ L\ \cdot\ i$ and that the lattice relation $\mathrm{LatticeRel}$ holds, for the reduced datum of $t.\mathrm{XbarS}\ g$ and the integer $t.n$, between the rigidification map $t.\mathrm{rigidNum}$ (the composite of $r_\Phi$ with base change from $D_\Phi$ through $\mathrm{PhibarS}$ and then along $\rho$) and the reduction $t.\mathrm{etaRed}$ of $(D.\mathrm{nVarpi})^i z$, against $p^i \cdot v$. Lastly $\Theta$ is an additive map from the Cartier module of $t.\mathrm{XS}\ g$ to that of $t'.\mathrm{XS}\ g$, assumed bijective (`hΘ`) and compatible with: Frobenius (`hΘF`), the integral Verschiebung (`hΘV`), the action of $W(\mathrm{Baway}\,(1:\kappa))$ (`hΘW`), the endomorphisms $\mathrm{actEnd}\ a$ for $a \in \mathrm{Zp2}\,p$ (`hΘa`), and the endomorphism $\varpi$ (`hΘPi`); and `hΘη` requires that $\Theta$ induces $\theta_\eta$ on classes of the form $\mathrm{nMk}(m,0)$: whenever $\mathrm{nMk}(m,0)$ lies in $D.\mathrm{etaPiece}\ L\ \cdot\ i$, the image of that class under $\theta_\eta$ equals $D'.\mathrm{nMk}(\Theta m, 0)$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a formal group law $\Psi$ of dimension $2$ over $\kappa$, a proof that $\Psi$ is commutative, homomorphisms of formal groups $\rho_\kappa : \Psi \to t.X.F$ and $\rho'_\kappa : \Psi \to t'.X.F$, and an additive map $\theta$ from the Cartier module of $t.X.F$ to the Cartier module of $t'.X.F$, such that all of the following hold: the reduction of the power series of $\rho_\kappa$ modulo the ideal generated by $p$ in $\kappa$ equals $t.\rho$; the reduction of the power series of $\rho'_\kappa$ modulo that ideal equals $t'.\rho$; $\theta$ is bijective; $\theta$ commutes with Frobenius; $\theta$ commutes with the characteristic-$p$ Verschiebung $\mathrm{verschiebung}$ (not only with the integral Verschiebung occurring in the hypothesis on $\Theta$); $\theta$ commutes with the homothety by $a$ for every $a \in \kappa$; $\theta$ intertwines the endomorphisms $t.X.\mathrm{actEnd}\ a$ and $t'.X.\mathrm{actEnd}\ a$ for every $a \in \mathrm{Zp2}\,p$; $\theta$ intertwines $t.X.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$ and $t'.X.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$; and there is a natural number $c$ such that for every $f$ in the Cartier module of $\Psi$,
--   $$\theta\bigl(\mathrm{map}\,\rho_\kappa\,(p^{\,c + t'.n} \cdot f)\bigr) = \mathrm{map}\,\rho'_\kappa\,(p^{\,c + t.n} \cdot f).$$
--
--   This is the transport step in the Cartier-theoretic comparison underlying the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: a Cartier-module isomorphism on the localised model, compatible with the $\eta$-coordinates of the two rigidifications, is converted into an isomorphism of Cartier modules of $t.X$ and $t'.X$ over $\kappa$ itself, together with formal-group homomorphisms from a common $\Psi$ lifting $t.\rho$ and $t'.\rho$ and matched up to a power of $p$. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.SpecialFormal.Rigidified.IsCartierQuadruple.exists_bijective_cartierModule_map_nsmul_eq_of_isIsomorphic_of_isAlgClosed_of_lieZero_le_ker`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.SpecialFormal.Rigidified.IsCartierQuadruple.exists_bijective_cartierModule_map_nsmul_eq_of_isIsomorphic_of_isAlgClosed_of_lieZero_le_ker), which deduces the isomorphism from an isomorphism of the associated Drinfeld data.
